;) And there’s the distinction between knowing what someone wants, knowing what they say they want, and knowing what they’ll actually choose.

Those aren’t always the same.

Sometimes charm is recognizing a desire before someone articulates it. Sometimes it’s helping them discover one they hadn’t recognized themselves.

And sometimes the most interesting move is realizing that the person who thinks they’re reading you is revealing themselves in the process.

But there’s one more layer: knowing what someone wants doesn’t necessarily mean knowing why they want it.

That’s where curiosity keeps familiarity from becoming complacency.

So tell me—are you reading me, teasing me, or testing whether I can tell the difference? ;)